"Internal Error ()\nciteturn9view0 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://kylewhyte.seas.umich.edu/climate-justice/\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 1\nL0: Failed to fetch https://kylewhyte.seas.umich.edu/climate-justice/: (400) Timeout fetching\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n (https://www.ubcpress.ca/asset/12466/1/9780774811866.pdf)\nciteturn9view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 18; Source: click({\"ref_id\":\"turn7view0\",\"id\":15}); Total lines: 507\nL0@P0: INTRODUCTION\nL1@P0: The Stubborn Particulars of Voice\nL2@P0: Glaciers and their intangible connections to recent human history provide\nL3@P0: this book’s unifying theme. I draw on climate histories, colonial records,\nL4@P0: Aboriginal oral traditions from the Yukon and Alaska,1 and a personal fas\u0002cination with the imaginative force that glaciers exert on regional histories\nL5@P0: in northwestern North America. The icefields in question flow from the\nL6@P0: craggy mountain spine where Canada and the United States meet at their\nL7@P0: least-known boundary, the Saint Elias Mountain divide. Far below that\nL8@P0: serrated crest, Tlingit peoples on the Gulf of Alaska coast and inland speak\u0002ers of Athapaskan languages came to know this glacial landscape during\nL9@P0: successive generations. Their experiences reinforced a vision that humans\nL10@P0: and nature mutually make and maintain the habitable world, a view now\nL11@P0: echoed by environmental historians.\nL12@P0: Glaciers appear as actors in this book. In accounts we will hear from\nL13@P0: Athapaskan and Tlingit oral tradition, glaciers take action and respond to\nL14@P0: their surroundings. They are sensitive to smells and they listen. They make\nL15@P0: moral judgments and they punish infractions. Some elders who know them\nL16@P0: well describe them as both animate (endowed with life) and as animating\nL17@P0: (giving life to) landscapes they inhabit. Glaciers also enter accounts left by\nL18@P0: early North Atlantic visitors who wrote in a different idiom and brought\nL19@P0: distinctive environmental visions to their interpretations.\nL20@P0: English is a language rich in nouns but lacking verb forms that distin\u0002guish animate from inanimate subjects. Both Athapaskan and Tlingit lan\u0002guages have comparatively fewer nouns but are verb-rich and hence often\nL21@P0: define landscape in terms of its actions. The Tlingit noun for glacier is\nL22@P0: sit’; in Southern Tutchone, an inland Athapaskan language spoken on\nL23@P0: the Canadian side of the mountains, the words are tän shäw (tän “ice” +\nL24@P0: shäw “big”) or tän shi (beneath the front of glacier). Both languages\nL25@P0-1: emphasize activity and motion, making no distinction between animate4 Introduction\nL26@P1: and inanimate. In Athapaskan languages, you know something is animate\nL27@P1: if the verb signals that it has the power to act on other things or to move,\nL28@P1: and actions are often attributed to entities, such as glaciers, that English\nL29@P1: speakers would define as inanimate. Linguistic anthropologists discuss this\nL30@P1: “enlivening” influence of the Tlingit verbs and how this results in “action\u0002oriented naming.”2\nL31@P1:  In Athapaskan languages, an elaborate system of verb\nL32@P1: stems means that different stems are used for singular, dual, and plural verb\nL33@P1: forms. Furthermore, directional clues situate characters and their actions\nL34@P1: so that if a speaker is orienting herself to a glacier, for instance, one verb\nL35@P1: stem will be used to indicate travelling upstream, another for going down\u0002stream, another for crossing away from a point of reference, another for\nL36@P1: crossing toward a point of reference, and so on.3 Clearly, the varied ways of\nL37@P1: speaking about glaciers are sometimes difficult to capture in English.\nL38@P1: “A country that listens,” as anthropologist Elizabeth Povinelli has shown,\nL39@P1: was not unfamiliar in Europe before conditions underpinning the Indus\u0002trial Revolution began to spill over into everyday lives, language, and be\u0002liefs. It is not surprising, she observes, that we now find it difficult to believe\nL40@P1: that rocks, mountains, and other landscape features like glaciers might lis\u0002ten, when the very conditions of the Western material and cultural world\nL41@P1: are underpinned by language that rejects that possibility.4\nL42@P1:  The question,\nL43@P1: Do Glaciers Listen? is less straightforward than it may at first seem.\nL44@P1: An enduring theme of oral traditions told in northwestern North America\nL45@P1: is the inclusion of nature in human affairs. One of my objectives in the\nL46@P1: following chapters is to provide an account of how interpretations of natu\u0002ral, social, and cultural worlds became gradually disaggregated in a place\nL47@P1: where they were formerly viewed as unified, and to examine consequences\nL48@P1: of that fragmentation. Many disciplines are re-evaluating reciprocal rela\u0002tionships between humankind and the natural world, and some now iden\u0002tify nature as a category of social analysis as important as (and entangled\nL49@P1: with) class, race, and gender.5 My aim in this book, then, is twofold. First,\nL50@P1: I argue that local knowledge is never crudely encapsulated in closed tradi\u0002tions, but is produced during human encounters, rather than “discovered.”\nL51@P1: It is dynamic and complex, and it often links biophysical and social proc\u0002esses. Second, I trace how long-contested views of nature weave uneasily\nL52@P1: through a history of encounters among humans and glaciers and into con\u0002temporary discussions about Protected Areas, parks, and a new World Her\u0002itage site that now encompasses the region. While my focus here is on a\nL53@P1: very specific corner of the globe, I contend that themes discussed in the\nL54@P1: following chapters, by no means limited to this location, speak to other\nL55@P1-2: arenas of colonial encounter. In that sense, I hope that this work may con\u0002tribute to what Michel Foucault once called “histories of the present.”The Stubborn Particulars of Voice 5\nL56@P2: The stories I trace in this book begin during the late stages of a period\nL57@P2: some scientists refer to as the Little Ice Age. Spanning roughly AD 1550-\nL58@P2: 1850, this Little Ice Age was characterized by cooler and more variable\nL59@P2: temperatures over much of the globe, with pronounced consequences at\nL60@P2: high latitude. Jean Grove, the physical geographer who coined this term\nL61@P2: for an interval of global cooling between the Middle Ages and the early\nL62@P2: twentieth century, traces some of its slipperiness in her classic volume The\nL63@P2: Little Ice Age, and notes that chronologies vary from region to region.\nL64@P2: Archaeologist Brian Fagan’s recent book of the same title suggests dates of\nL65@P2: 1300-1850 for Western Europe, but he also notes that scientists strongly\nL66@P2: disagree about dates.6\nL67@P2:  In the Pacific Northwest, the years between 1550\nL68@P2: and 1900 are commonly cited.\nL69@P2: In Europe, French historian Emmanuel LeRoy Ladurie traces records\nL70@P2: from medieval archives that chronicle the destruction of European villages\nL71@P2: by advancing glaciers during the Little Ice Age. Descriptions abound of\nL72@P2: “great and horrible glacier[s] of great and incalculable volume which can\nL73@P2: promise nothing but the destruction of houses and lands which still re\u0002main.”7 Terrified sixteenth-century villagers entered glacial caves with\nL74@P2: swords drawn, and they mounted crosses at the edge of terminal moraines\nL75@P2: in vain attempts to halt these advances. Volcanic activity also enhanced\nL76@P2: northern hemispheric cooling. Evidence from temperate regions of North\nL77@P2: America and Europe demonstrates that the summer of 1816 – the year\nL78@P2: after the volcanic eruption of Tambora in Java – was exceptionally cold.\nL79@P2: Reports from Connecticut, southern England, and Switzerland show that\nL80@P2: it was the coldest year for which records exist.8 At higher latitudes, the\nL81@P2: impact of volcanic dust in the upper atmosphere may have been even more\nL82@P2: significant. Romantic poets were especially captivated by Little Ice Age\nL83@P2: changes and drew on opportune cultural metaphors to portray expanding\nL84@P2: glaciers. Samuel Coleridge wrote what he called a devotional poem, “Hymn\nL85@P2: before Sunrise,” addressing glaciers at Chamonix as “motionless torrents,\nL86@P2: silent cataracts.”9\nL87@P2:  Victor Hugo’s portrayal of Mer de Glace was more ag\u0002gressive, “a bent arm [that] overhangs and hurls down great blocks of\nL88@P2: marble whiteness.”10 John Turner’s ruddy skies may have mirrored effects\nL89@P2: of volcanic dust.\nL90@P2: Glaciers also inspired aesthetic forms passed on orally and in writing from\nL91@P2: the Pacific Northwest, where global cooling was accompanied by signifi\u0002cant glacier activity on coastal shores and inland mountain passes. The\nL92@P2: icefields supported by the Saint Elias Mountains are the largest non-polar\nL93@P2: icefields in the world and they include a significant number of glaciers that\nL94@P2: surge quite independently of climate. Scientists now think that surges are\nL95@P2-3: triggered by subsurface deformation – processes involving water and6 Introduction\nL96@P3: Figure 1 Trapridge Glacier, in the Saint Elias Mountains, is one of approximately\nL97@P3: two hundred surging glaciers in northwestern North America. Trapridge Glacier\nL98@P3: Project Archives\nL99@P3: subglacial deposits, or till, under high pressure. Surging glaciers differ from\nL100@P3: the torrents and cataracts that inspired Coleridge in Europe, being neither\nL101@P3: motionless nor silent. They may advance without warning after years of\nL102@P3: stability, sometimes several kilometres, and as we will see in following chap\u0002ters, they frequently create ice-dammed lakes that build up and burst out\nL103@P3: with catastrophic consequences when the ice eventually thins and the dam\nL104@P3: breaks. The impact of glaciers on regional history, I suggest, lies not simply\nL105@P3: in their immense physical presence but also in their contributions to social\nL106@P3: imagination.\nL107@P3: Concerns about global climate change are giving glaciers new meaning\nL108@P3: for many people who may previously have considered them eternally fro\u0002zen, safely distant, and largely inert. Most of the world’s glaciers now seem\nL109@P3: to be melting rather than reproducing themselves, becoming a new kind of\nL110@P3: endangered species, a cryospheric weather vane for potential natural and\nL111@P3-4: social upheaval. Encounters with glaciers during times of rapid environ-The Stubborn Particulars of Voice 7\nL112@P4: mental change produce diverse interpretations. In the Andes, for instance,\nL113@P4: anthropologist Michael Sallnow has documented an age-old practice where\nL114@P4: thousands of contemporary pilgrims make their way up to the towering\nL115@P4: glaciers of Mount Sinakara during the fiesta of Señor de Qoyllur Rit’i.\nL116@P4: They light candles, reclaim crosses placed there a few days earlier, and carve\nL117@P4: out blocks of ice and laboriously carry them back down to a sanctuary. Or\nL118@P4: so they did, that is, until the 1990s when this practice was discontinued in\nL119@P4: order to stop the glacier from losing even more of its ice.11 In Asia,\nL120@P4: geoscientist Ken Hewitt first heard accounts of gendered ice and artificial\nL121@P4: glaciers in 1961 when he began working in the Karakoram Range. In Oc\u0002tober of that year, a small group of local men bearing baskets lined with\nL122@P4: leather and straw began filling them with ice from the snout of the Biafo\nL123@P4: Glacier where he was working. He learned that specialists had determined\nL124@P4: that fetching ice from this glacier to “plant” at the opposite-gendered gla\u0002cier overlooking their village could alleviate a drought affecting the village.\nL125@P4: The village headman gave the young scientists permission to visit the site,\nL126@P4: where they found a somewhat active remnant of a cirque glacier covered\nL127@P4: with broken rock. They were shown a hole where some half-dozen alter\u0002nate layers of “male” and “female” ice were laid, each layer covered with\nL128@P4: special twigs, straw, and cloth.12 And in his ongoing historical work in Pe\u0002ru’s Cordillera Blanca, Mark Carey reports that in one glacier location,\nL129@P4: campesinos recently confiscated measuring devices scientists were using to\nL130@P4: monitor changes in glaciers because they suspected that the instruments\nL131@P4: might be causing a drought.13 Imaginative possibilities from such differing\nL132@P4: locations and historical periods suggest that glaciers (and stories about them)\nL133@P4: may provide one lens through which to assess current cross-disciplinary\nL134@P4: discussions about the relevance of local knowledge to environmental poli\u0002cies.14 As historian Richard White observes, there is a tangible physical world\nL135@P4: out there that sometimes affirms but often mocks the representations we\nL136@P4: design to constrain it.15\nL137@P4: Romantic poets, Tlingit elders, scientists, and linguists, such as those\nL138@P4: cited in the opening epigraph, bring contrasting approaches to understand\u0002ing climate change, and they all deserve attention. Percy Shelley wrote his\nL139@P4: poem “Mont Blanc”16 when he was visiting the Alps in 1816, the reputed\nL140@P4: “year without a summer” when Mary Shelley also wrote Frankenstein, send\u0002ing her protagonist deep into Alpine glaciers during his tormented strug\u0002gle to become human. Deikinaak’w, a senior Tlingit orator, spoke at length\nL141@P4: with ethnographer John Swanton in 1904 and reflected on accounts he\nL142@P4-5: had heard about glacier surges on the Alsek River during the previous cen\u0002tury.17 Wallace Broecker, a prestigious geophysicist from Columbia Uni\u0002versity’s Lamont-Doherty Earth Observatory, was addressing a New York8 Introduction\nL143@P5: Times readership concerned about future climate change at the end of the\nL144@P5: twentieth century.18 Daniel Tlen, linguist and member of Kluane First Na\u0002tion, records oral histories with elders in the southern Yukon and teaches\nL145@P5: at Yukon College in Whitehorse.19\nL146@P5: I first encountered these glaciers in stories told by elderly First Nation\nL147@P5: women who spent their lives inland from the Saint Elias Mountains in what\nL148@P5: is now the Yukon Territory. Three women in particular, Kitty Smith and\nL149@P5: Annie Ned, both born in the early 1890s, and Angela Sidney, born in 1902,\nL150@P5: unexpectedly forced me to think more carefully about glaciers. Their extra\u0002ordinary lives spanned the century that began with the 1896-98 Klondike\nL151@P5: gold rush and ended just before the turn of the millennium. In the 1970s,\nL152@P5: they began our conversations with chronicles of ancestors who had lived\nL153@P5: within the shadow of these mountains as glaciers advanced and receded\nL154@P5: during the Little Ice Age, and whose stories flowed into their own. They\nL155@P5: spoke about glaciers familiarly and often by name, well aware of their dan\u0002gers, and they integrated accounts of glacier behaviour into narratives of\nL156@P5: their own experiences.\nL157@P5: The women I knew portrayed glaciers as conscious and responsive to\nL158@P5: humans. Glaciers, they insisted, are wilful, sometimes capricious, easily ex\u0002cited by human intemperance but equally placated by quick-witted human\nL159@P5: responses. Glaciers engage all the senses. I was informed, for instance, of\nL160@P5: firm taboos against “cooking with grease” near glaciers, which are offended\nL161@P5: by such smells. I heard concerns expressed about hikers who embark on\nL162@P5: overnight camping trips in Kluane National Park and might inadvertently\nL163@P5: trigger a glacier surge of ear-splitting force and great danger by cooking\nL164@P5: bacon for breakfast. Other stories dramatize consequences of improper\nL165@P5: treatment of cooked food, especially fat, that might grow into a glacier\nL166@P5: overnight. The narratives sometimes seemed to be less about actual gla\u0002ciers than about the imaginative possibilities they provide. Glaciers por\u0002trayed as sentient beings in these stories seemed to be, in the felicitous\nL167@P5: phrase of French anthropologist Claude Lévi-Strauss, “good to think with.”\nL168@P5: What most clearly differentiates the stories these women tell from written\nL169@P5: accounts left by early travellers whose accounts we will read in later chap\u0002ters is the way Yukon storytellers merge natural histories of landscape with\nL170@P5: social histories and pursue their analogies in a local human ecology.\nL171@P5: What happens during colonial encounters when matters of locality con\u0002front unfamiliar practices of exploration? I approach this as a problem in\nL172@P5: the anthropology of encounter that can be retrospectively investigated\nL173@P5: through narrative traces, which accumulate from such meetings. The idea\nL174@P5: of encounter seems especially useful because of what it reveals about scale\nL175@P5-6: and subjectivity. Initially, the actors in this region were relatively few, andThe Stubborn Particulars of Voice 9\nL176@P6: their motives, intentions, and imaginings can be traced partially in diaries\nL177@P6: and reports, but also in orally narrated stories still told. These stories may\nL178@P6: originate in the past, but they continue to resonate with contemporary\nL179@P6: debates about history, science, and colonial practices as well as with current\nL180@P6: struggles surrounding environmentalism, land rights, nationhood, and\nL181@P6: national parks.\nL182@P6: Anthropologists have recently addressed the need for more differenti\u0002ated portrayals of colonial encounters. A postcolonial metanarrative that\nL183@P6: depicts coherent, homogeneous colonialism as an intractable template with\nL184@P6: predictable outcomes, they point out, now seems too globalizing and too\nL185@P6: ahistorical. Such representations of history also err in their apparent relega\u0002tion of colonialism safely to the past.20 Geographers point to the intellec\u0002tual sleight of hand that can occur when urban scholars, settled in universities\nL186@P6: far from places they study, cautiously eschew speaking directly about “Oth\u0002ers,” yet casually bring a shaky sense of the colonial setting (without his\u0002tory or context) to their analysis of European imperial practices, presumably\nL187@P6: on such Others’ behalf. Postcolonial literature inclines increasingly to theo\u0002retical concerns that seem ever more distant from detailed knowledge of\nL188@P6: local practices and power relations on the ground. This underlying\nL189@P6: universalism can be interpreted as refuelling an intellectual imperialism that\nL190@P6: once again presents Europe as the key historical agent, as though no other\nL191@P6: actors existed. Yet the aftermaths of colonialism are always local.21 Taking\nL192@P6: up these themes, I focus on early encounters where transfers of knowledge\nL193@P6: seem to have occurred on both sides, before the balance of power in such\nL194@P6: exchanges shifted and state infrastructure made “difference” a rationale for\nL195@P6: inequality. Because narratives energize both exploration practices and local\nL196@P6: meanings, it is worth paying attention to what such stories accomplish,\nL197@P6: how they move, and why they persist – my ongoing preoccupation in this\nL198@P6: book.\nL199@P6: Environmental Change, Local Knowledge, and Human Encounters\nL200@P6: This work will, I hope, contribute to literature about environmental change,\nL201@P6: local knowledge, and human encounters – three themes often central to\nL202@P6: historical struggles in places depicted as “remote” despite long entangle\u0002ments with world markets. Changing environments figure prominently in\nL203@P6: oral and written accounts passed on from late stages of the Little Ice Age.\nL204@P6: Environmental historians advise us to examine nature as a continuing force\nL205@P6: in history, not in a determining role but as one actor in relation to others.22\nL206@P6: Local knowledge, as I use the term, refers to tacit knowledge embodied in\nL207@P6: life experiences and reproduced in everyday behaviour and speech. In the\nL208@P6-7: early twenty-first century, local knowledge has become a commonsense10 Introduction\nL209@P7: term, couched in acronyms like TEK (traditional ecological knowledge) or\nL210@P7: IK (indigenous knowledge), gaining new visibility in management-science\nL211@P7: studies, but too often depicted as static, timeless, and hermetically sealed.23\nL212@P7: A historical approach reveals how socially situated but also how porous\nL213@P7: knowledge practices are. As the field of science studies shows, all knowl\u0002edge is incontrovertibly local. Encounters come in many forms. We hear of\nL214@P7: human encounters among strangers, but also of encounters between those\nL215@P7: same people and transforming landscapes. Stories that participants tell orally\nL216@P7: or in writing about their experiences, in turn, encounter successive audi\u0002ences whose interpretations of what they hear or read are shaped by their\nL217@P7: own contemporary concerns. In some cases, meetings between humans or\nL218@P7: landscapes may generate insights on all sides. But encounters, as Gillian\nL219@P7: Beer notes, do not guarantee understanding and may instead emphasize\nL220@P7: what is incommensurate.24 Such themes connect chapters in this book.\nL221@P7: Briefly, my argument is this. In northwestern North America, two fun\u0002damental processes that are often discussed independently coincided in the\nL222@P7: late eighteenth and nineteenth centuries: geophysical changes associated\nL223@P7: with late stages of the Little Ice Age and European colonial incursions.\nL224@P7: Raymond Williams’s observation that the concept of nature always con\u0002tains an extraordinary amount of human history is especially relevant here.25\nL225@P7: When glaciers on the North Pacific coast began to melt and recede, they\nL226@P7: released new lands that were gradually occupied by ancestors of contempo\u0002rary Tlingit and Athapaskan peoples. But late stages of this Little Ice Age\nL227@P7: also coincided with European mercantile forays, initially on coastal shores\nL228@P7: and eventually across mountains into the interior uplands. A time of sig\u0002nificant geophysical change, then, coincided with dramatic social upheaval,\nL229@P7: causing both readjustments and realignments among resident peoples and\nL230@P7: the permanent problem of powerful strangers who came to stay. Natural\nL231@P7: and cultural histories, in other words, are objectively entangled in this place.\nL232@P7: Second, interpretations by participants struggling to make sense of both\nL233@P7: geophysical changes and social encounters often diverged dramatically.\nL234@P7: Eighteenth-century European visitors were especially excited by ideas of\nL235@P7: an inanimate nature subject to empirical investigation and measurement.\nL236@P7: They were heartened by the possibility that their Enlightenment categories\nL237@P7: and scientific instruments might help them to pry nature from culture.\nL238@P7: They imported these ideas to places like the Arctic and Africa, where local\nL239@P7: cultural meanings were framed very differently. Visitors wrote about gla\u0002ciers, for instance, as natural spaces – pristine, wild, and remote from hu\u0002man influence, “on the verge of the world” in the words of one early French\nL240@P7: voyager, Jean-François de La Pérouse.26 This local North Atlantic idea of a\nL241@P7-8: self-evident divide neatly separating natural from social phenomena con-The Stubborn Particulars of Voice 11\nL242@P8: trasted sharply with Tlingit and Athapaskan understandings. Their oral tra\u0002ditions frame glaciers as intensely social spaces where human behaviour,\nL243@P8: especially casual hubris or arrogance, can trigger dramatic and unpleasant\nL244@P8: consequences in the physical world. In other words, Tlingit and Athapaskan\nL245@P8: oral traditions explore the connections between nature and culture as care\u0002fully as early exploration projects tried to disentangle them. Later, we will\nL246@P8: hear parallel distinctions from Central Africa, where jungles (like glaciers)\nL247@P8: evoke local connections with ancestors and historical knowledge about\nL248@P8: particular forest places. There, too, local visions contrast sharply with an\nL249@P8: iconography of jungles and savagery that provided the groundwork for\nL250@P8: colonial expansion.\nL251@P8: Third, conflicting depictions of glaciers in oral histories and historical\nL252@P8: records have twenty-first-century consequences. They seem to typify or\nL253@P8: even model classic and continuing struggles over cultural meanings repli\u0002cated in contemporary debates. The consequences of what Bruno Latour\nL254@P8: calls this “Great Divide”27 differentiating nature from culture continue to\nL255@P8: cascade internationally through debates about environmentalism, biodiver\u0002sity, global climate change, and indigenous rights. It is the contemporary\nL256@P8: anthropocentric implications of these historical understandings that espe\u0002cially interest me. Anthropologist Paul Nadasdy argues that a growing\nL257@P8: number of terms crucial to ongoing land claims negotiations, wildlife\u0002management debates, and environmental conflicts in northwestern Canada\nL258@P8: – terms like “land,” “hunting,” “resources,” and “property” that may ini\u0002tially seem straightforward – are actually fundamentally contested. All par\u0002ties may agree on their importance, yet negotiating parties may mean very\nL259@P8: different things when they use the same terms. Participants predictably\nL260@P8: take their own meanings to represent “common sense,” but the more pow\u0002erful parties regularly build their meanings into legal agreements.28 Dis\u0002tinct though glaciers may seem from such daily negotiations, observations\nL261@P8: about their behaviour and qualities resonate with current debates about\nL262@P8: local environmental knowledge. Glaciers’ temporal and spatial strangeness\nL263@P8: for most of us may actually enable local ideas about them to better resist\nL264@P8: metropolitan interpretive schemes.\nL265@P8: Fourth and finally, these themes all concern memory, stability, and change\nL266@P8: in human affairs. Anthropologists, geographers, and historians have dem\u0002onstrated the enduring power of landscape features to act as points of ref\u0002erence for communicating tacit knowledge. Humans persist in transforming\nL267@P8: seemingly neutral spaces into places of significance. A growing body of\nL268@P8-9: research about social memory argues that landscapes are places of remem\u0002brance and that culturally significant landforms may provide a kind of ar\u0002chive where memories can be mentally stored.29 In the Saint Elias Mountains,12 Introduction\nL269@P9: though, we can see also how changing landscape features, such as fluctuat\u0002ing glaciers, have provided imaginative grist for comprehending and inter\u0002preting shifting social circumstances. Orally narrated stories do indeed\nL270@P9: provide empirical observations about geophysical changes and their conse\u0002quences. But they also demonstrate how glaciers provide substantive mate\u0002rial for evaluating changes wrought by colonial histories.\nL271@P9: It is hardly surprising that local knowledge in the notebooks of Euro\u0002American scientists and travellers often differs from that passed on in Ab\u0002original traditions, yet the lines are not always so sharp. Ideas develop in\nL272@P9: relationship with others and emerge from encounters in particular places at\nL273@P9: specific times. The various approaches to knowledge brought to bear on\nL274@P9: these northwest borderlands contribute perspectives on Canadian history\nL275@P9: from a location that often remains a mere footnote in canonical accounts\nL276@P9: proceeding from east to west, with only occasional glances north.\nL277@P9: North America’s Towering Ridge\nL278@P9: Encounters discussed in the following chapters centre on a location that\nL279@P9: has long been portrayed both as physically remote and internationally con\u0002nected. Its axis is the most inaccessible region of Canada’s northwest bor\u0002der with the United States, and it is also the spine of a recently designated\nL280@P9: UNESCO international World Heritage site. The precise location of this\nL281@P9: international border is not especially well known to residents of either\nL282@P9: Canada or the United States, and following its tortuous route can be dis\u0002orienting (see Map 1). The southern perimeter crosses Dixon Entrance,\nL283@P9: skimming the southern edge of Prince of Wales Island. It reaches the main\u0002land at the Portland Canal and swings northward to the head of this nar\u0002row fiord. From there, the border follows the mountain crest in a relatively\nL284@P9: smooth contour along the coast to a point near the head of Lynn Canal,\nL285@P9: where it veers precipitously southwest toward the pinnacle of Mount\nL286@P9: Fairweather, retrieving a triangle of British Columbia for Canada. It then\nL287@P9: clings to the jagged mountain ridge that separates the high inland plateau\nL288@P9: from the Gulf of Alaska for more than two hundred kilometres before\nL289@P9: turning sharply north at the 141st meridian on its unswerving course to\nL290@P9: the Arctic Ocean.\nL291@P9: I focus here on a restricted portion of that border, the part anchored by\nL292@P9: the Saint Elias Mountains separating the coastal Gulf of Alaska from the\nL293@P9: high-country Yukon Plateau. In cross-section, this boundary lurches from\nL294@P9-10: peak to peak as it traverses some of North America’s highest peaks. Cross\u0002ing enormous icefields, it typifies immoderation. Delineated more conven\u0002tionally on a two-dimensional map, it resembles a child’s concentrated effortsThe Stubborn Particulars of Voice 13\nL295@P10: Map 1 The northwest Canada-United States border. A World Heritage site\nL296@P10: (shaded) now straddles this border, encompassing four parks. Over the years, park\nL297@P10: names have altered with status changes. In 2005 the parks on the Canadian side\nL298@P10: are named Kluane National Park and Reserve and Tatshenshini-Alsek Provincial\nL299@P10: Park; and those in the United States are officially known as Wrangell-Saint Elias\nL300@P10-11: National Park and Preserve and Glacier Bay National Park and Preserve.14 Introduction\nL301@P11: to connect unruly dots. The dimensions of this boundary were first imag\u0002ined in the 1825 Anglo-Russian Treaty, but plotting its actual location\nL302@P11: took almost a century. The southern extension was given the form of a pan\u0002handle (to protect Russian settlements) with the proposed line to follow\nL303@P11: the mountain divide, except where that height of land swung inland more\nL304@P11: than ten marine leagues (55.6 kilometres) from the sea; in such cases the\nL305@P11: boundary was to adhere to this ten-league measurement. Canada’s efforts\nL306@P11: to locate the height of land as close as possible to the ocean and to capture\nL307@P11: a North Pacific port clashed with US determination to push the boundary\nL308@P11: as far inland as possible. Some of the twists and turns of those negotiations\nL309@P11: are discussed in Chapter 7.\nL310@P11: I am curious about the Saint Elias Mountains for several reasons. First,\nL311@P11: for many years I have heard a good deal about how this region is remem\u0002bered in indigenous oral histories. Second, as we will see in Chapter 1,\nL312@P11: there is a long record of scientific research here, at high altitude and high\nL313@P11: latitude, that makes it a key site for twenty-first century climate change\nL314@P11: research, and hence a site from which scientists now claim legitimacy to\nL315@P11: speak. Third, the World Heritage site spanning the Alaska/Canada bor\u0002der encompasses four parks: Kluane National Park and Reserve and\nL316@P11: Tatshenshini-Alsek Provincial Park on the Canadian side; Wrangell-Saint\nL317@P11: Elias National Park and Preserve and Glacier Bay National Park and Pre\u0002serve in the United States. Here, unproblematized representations of na\u0002ture as “aesthetic landscape,” “endangered/pristine wilderness,” or even\nL318@P11: as a giant jungle-gym for eco-tourists now compete with conceptions held\nL319@P11: by Aboriginal residents who once lived and hunted in these parks. If natu\u0002ral and cultural history were enmeshed here in the past, this also seems to\nL320@P11: be the case today.\nL321@P11: Despite its iconic status as America’s “wildest” wilderness30 and its al\u0002leged distance from metropolitan affairs, this region was already a hub of\n"
